    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/tv/own/owntv/features/sports/SportsMatchScreen.kt",
      "content": "Before this change, a new match always got a freshly composed `SportsMatchScreen`, because `pendingMatch` was read only once, on entering the section. Now `SportsScreen` collects `pendingMatch` all the time, so the goal toast can call `open(m)` while this page is already showing. The page then switches matches without being recomposed from scratch, and it keeps state from the old match:\n- `focused` is `remember {}` with no key (unlike `revealed`, which is keyed on `m.key`). While the box is empty, the right column draws `focused ?: shown.firstOrNull()`, so it shows the previous match's channel name. The OK hint (`okOn`) is also computed from that old channel.\n- `listState` keeps the old match's scroll position.\n- `rowFocus` keeps a `FocusRequester` for every channel ever shown, so it grows with each match.\n- The focus effect is keyed on `state.search::class` and `isNotEmpty()`. These may be the same for both matches, so the effect may not run again and focus can stay on a row that no longer exists.\n\nFix: key this per-match state on `m.key`, e.g. `remember(m.key) { mutableStateOf\u003cWatchOption?\u003e(null) }` for `focused` and `remember(m.key) { ChannelRowFocus() }`, and reset the list scroll when the match changes. `m` is only available after the `page ?: return` early return, so move this state below that line or reset it in a `LaunchedEffect(m.key)`.",
      "existing_code": "    val listState = androidx.compose.foundation.lazy.rememberLazyListState()\n    val rowFocus = remember { ChannelRowFocus() }",
      "start_line": 136,
      "end_line": 137,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
